  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mapping Migrant Presence and Needs: Providing Evidence to Better Protect Migrants in Jordan and Lebanon</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Both Jordan and Lebanon host significant numbers of international migrants and refugees. While most of this population, in both countries, are forcibly displaced,  the circumstances of migrants, including migrant workers (MWs), have also increasingly raised concern among observers in both countries, despite a significant gap in the consistent availability of quantitative, as well as qualitative data related to this population. This concern has arisen out of known systemic challenges to MWs accessing services and claiming rights, as well as an environment that provides limited protection from potential exploitation and abuse, particularly for Migrant Domestic Workers (MDWs) who are known to be especially exposed to abuse and exploitation.   The proposed project will contribute to evidence-based governmental and non-governmental efforts to protect the rights of migrant workers (MWs), and migrant domestic workers (MDWs) in particular (who are predominantly women) and provide them with protection and assistance by taking the first step towards addressing the limited availability of adequate data and information about this population in Jordan. The project will build on IOM efforts in other locations, particularly in Lebanon, to map migrant presence and needs by implementing a set of adapted and tailored methods and tools to collect, compile, and analyze quantitative and qualitative data that will be made available to relevant key governmental and non-governmental stakeholders. This data will also be inclusive of migrant voices, and data collection will be implemented with the significant engagement of the intended target group. The resulting findings and recommendations will provide key stakeholders with a picture of MW profiles, disaggregated by age, gender, and nationality, with a focus on MDW profiles specifically in Jordan, needs and gaps, and behavioral trends, to inform more effective future initiatives to protect and assist this population, as well as inform advocacy for policy changes that will ultimately create a stronger protection environment for them. The project will also build on Lebanese accomplishments and support a targeted assessment to connect MWs with social protection services available in Lebanon.Disaggregated data will allow the project to explore the gender dimension of identified needs, gaps, and behavioral trends. Data analysis will, therefore, be undertaken with an eye toward contributing to addressing the gender-related disparities in the treatment and experiences of MWs. The project will also pay particular attention to incorporating and reflecting the MWs&apos; voices and envisions the engagement of members of relevant migrant communities to participate in data collection as engaged enumerators.</narrative>
  </description>
